'''Experience''' (commonly abbreviated as '''XP''' or '''Exp''') is the core progression metric in EvilQuest. As players train any of the 16 [[Skills|skills]]—such as fighting monsters, harvesting timber, mining precious ores, brewing herbal teas, casting transmutations, or overcoming agility obstacles—they gain experience points. Accumulating experience advances a skill's level, unlocking higher-tier equipment, new recipes, spells, and access to challenging content across the realm.

== Progression Mechanics ==
* '''Maximum Skill Level''': Every skill has a maximum level cap of '''150'''.
* '''Level 150 Requirement''': Reaching level 150 requires '''2,033,749,558 XP'''.
* '''Hard Experience Cap''': After reaching level 150, players can continue accumulating experience until reaching the absolute engine cap of '''2,147,483,647 XP''' (<math>2^{31} - 1</math>, the maximum value of a signed 32-bit integer). An additional 113,734,089 XP is required between level 150 and the hard cap.
* '''Starting Skill Levels''':
** [[Hitpoints]] starts at '''Level 10''' with '''1,154 XP'''.
** [[Light Magic]] and [[Dark Magic]] start at '''Level 0''' with '''0 XP'''.
** All other 13 skills start at '''Level 1''' with '''0 XP'''.
* '''Maximum Total Level''': Across all 16 skills, a fully maxed character achieves a total level of '''2,400''' (<math>16 \times 150</math>) and an ultimate total experience of '''34,359,738,352 XP''' (<math>16 \times 2,147,483,647</math>).

== Experience Formula ==
The exact cumulative experience required to reach any given level <math>L</math> (for <math>L \ge 2</math>) is calculated by the game engine using the formula:

:<math>\text{XP}(L) = \left\lfloor \frac{1}{4} \sum_{o=1}^{L-1} \lfloor o + 300 \cdot 2^{o/7} \rfloor \right\rfloor</math>

Where:
* <math>L</math> is the target level (<math>1 \le L \le 150</math>). Level 1 requires 0 XP.
* <math>o</math> is the summation index representing each prior level.
* In each step, <math>\lfloor o + 300 \cdot 2^{o/7} \rfloor</math> calculates the scaled slice for that tier.
* The cumulative sum is divided by 4 and rounded down (<math>\lfloor \dots \rfloor</math>).
* The experience required approximately doubles every '''7 levels'''.

The difference in experience required to advance from level <math>L-1</math> to level <math>L</math> is given by:

:<math>\Delta\text{XP}(L) = \text{XP}(L) - \text{XP}(L-1)</math>

